## The Bug

The condition `decoded.exp < Date.now() / 1000` uses strict less-than, so when `exp === now` the check is `false` — the token is accepted despite being expired. It should be `<=`.

There's a second issue: `jwt.verify()` already validates expiry and throws `TokenExpiredError` automatically, so the manual check is both wrong *and* redundant. The catch block swallows all errors with the same generic message, hiding the distinction between expired vs. malformed tokens.

## Fix

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function authMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'No token provided' });
  }

  try {
    // jwt.verify throws TokenExpiredError if exp <= now (inclusive), so the
    // manual exp check below is removed — it was both wrong and redundant.
    const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = decoded;
    next();
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }
    if (err.name === 'JsonWebTokenError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
    }
    // Unexpected errors (e.g. secret misconfiguration) should not leak details
    next(err);
  }
}

module.exports = authMiddleware;
```

## Key changes

| Issue | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Boundary condition | `exp < now` accepts `exp === now` | `jwt.verify` uses `exp <= now` (RFC 7519 §4.1.4 compliant) |
| Redundant manual check | Duplicated and wrong | Removed; trust `jsonwebtoken` |
| Error granularity | All errors → same 401 message | Expired vs. invalid distinguished |
| Unexpected errors | Silently swallowed | Forwarded to Express error handler via `next(err)` |

`jsonwebtoken` follows RFC 7519 which defines a token as expired when the current time is **at or after** `exp`, so letting the library handle it is both correct and simpler.
